-- title:
--   Cutoff error estimate, indicator on the first variable
-- statement:
--   Let $\Gamma$ be a configuration on $\mathbb{R}^d$, let $\alpha, \Lambda \in \mathbb{R}$, and let
--
--   $$k : \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d \to [0,\infty]$$
--
--   be a kernel satisfying the standing two-sided bound `KernelBounds Γ α Λ k`, which relates $k$ to the fractional kernel of order $\alpha$ with constant $\Lambda$ along the configuration $\Gamma$. Assume $d \ge 1$, $\delta > 0$, $0 < \alpha < 2$, that $f : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}$ is measurable, that $k$ is measurable as a function of the pair, and that $S \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ is measurable. Then
--
--   $$\iint_{\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d} \mathbf{1}_S(s)\,f(s)^2\,\min\!\Big(\frac{\|s-t\|^2}{\delta^2},\,1\Big)\,k(s,t)\;ds\,dt \;\le\; \Lambda\;\Big(\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \min\!\Big(\frac{\|u\|^2}{\delta^2},\,1\Big)\|u\|^{-d-\alpha}\,du\Big)\;\int_S f(s)^2\,ds .$$
--
--   Here $\mathbf{1}_S$ is the indicator of $S$ and the weight $\min(\|s-t\|^2/\delta^2, 1)$ is the factor produced by a Lipschitz cutoff of width $\delta$, which satisfies $(\chi(s)-\chi(t))^2 \le \min(\|s-t\|^2/\delta^2, 1)$.
--
--   The estimate is the $L^2$ cost of multiplying $f$ by a cutoff: the doubly-integrated error term is bounded by the $L^2$ mass of $f$ on $S$ times a purely dimensional constant, and the middle factor is finite precisely because $0 < \alpha < 2$. The point of the indicator is that the bound never sees $f$ outside $S$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** All values are in `ℝ≥0∞`; the real-valued product $\mathbf{1}_S(s)f(s)^2\min(\cdots)$ is transported by `ENNReal.ofReal` before being multiplied by $k(s,t)$. The constant $\Lambda$ enters as `ENNReal.ofReal Λ`, which loses nothing: `KernelBounds` contains the clause $1 \le \Lambda$, so $\Lambda$ is always at least one and `ENNReal.ofReal Λ` is a nonzero finite constant.
